Kafka is the authoritative history, while the PostgreSQL tables, native materialized view, and MongoDB collection are disposable read models that can be rebuilt. The ordinary `daily_revenue_view` table is maintained incrementally by projector code, like a Twitter-style precomputed per-user timeline maintained for fast reads. That approach gives the application precise control over per-event updates but also makes idempotency and bookkeeping application responsibilities. The native `daily_revenue_materialized` view instead stores the result of its defining PostgreSQL query over `orders_view` and changes only when `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW` runs. Its refresh recomputes a database-managed snapshot, which is simpler to define but can be more expensive than applying a small delta. A full replay is possible only while Kafka retention preserves every required event, and the single partition supplies total order at the cost of projector parallelism. PostgreSQL commits its tables and materialized-view refresh transactionally, but that transaction cannot include MongoDB, so a crash may leave stores at different offsets. Production projectors use event IDs, checkpoints, idempotent writes, and often versioned replacement projections to recover safely.

